#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119420 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/id #uuid "cbabcd64-6a33-552d-9f2f-704a9df44d1e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10906" :question/problem "The set $\\{ \\mathbf{a_1},\\mathbf{a_2} \\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be $\\mathbf{a}_2$ if\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/2fc772925dd6b793bdcb3a1eca96ea7e7f5fed17425f3500638340e047170c60.png)\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/f73258610652de9a656f3c4ed202241c58c98f18391b1393dabe62d92d9bef7d.png)" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/210a5c50467861cdfe48b46f2098212bbf387270b420a05394e318a0e0b48c74.png)\nTo see why, notice that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/91a29b57d32ed8c8e702f2988f7714ffc343ed6b4707b86b49b708ffad5c8bc8.png)\nIn other words, the vectors $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that $\\mathbf a_1$ and $\\mathbf a_2$ are linearly dependent, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{a}_1  +x_2  \\mathbf{a}_2 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas the solution $x_1=\\dfrac 1 3$ and $x_2=-1,$ which is different from $x_1=x_2=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119421 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/id #uuid "7b28b42f-0ac4-543e-baeb-51534aaad480" :question/math-academy-id "e-10907" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/4af8a5ea61752a394d3c3313e5cbda76ad1981d8d62573d533ece0db0dd38820.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\mathbf{a}_1 - \\mathbf{a}_2 -2 \\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}.$\n\n- $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3  = \\mathbf{0}$ only if $x_1=x_2=x_3 = 0.$\n\n- The set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3\\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{v}_3= \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4+0-4 \\\\ -1-1+2 \\\\ 0+0-14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2\\mathbf{a}_2 + x_3\\mathbf{a}_3 = \\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2, \\mathbf{a}_3 \\}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119422 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/id #uuid "3843dc13-0908-5d4c-adfe-836a928a1410" :question/math-academy-id "e-10908" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044078/assets/8d88cae0e40e7a377314e359086dc45fa0460c17ed55fe379cc15537886b2623.png)\n\nAssume that $x_1,x_2,$ and $x_3$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly independent.\n\n- The vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\nx_1 \\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + x_3 \\mathbf{v}_3 = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nis $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 6{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are $x_1$ and $x_2,$ while $x_3$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation $x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2+x_3\\mathbf{a}_3=\\mathbf{0}$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II is false, while statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "348be599-aa14-56e9-a0f5-522f07b90594" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322163} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b8a7f5-341a-59f9-9b79-9ca8e823ff72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322164} {:answer/id #uuid "cebf84bf-2cab-5237-aa21-3119bc62546a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322165} {:answer/id #uuid "d13a9ee8-7991-5dcc-aded-17b229330d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322166} {:answer/id #uuid "dc4dd780-9fd0-519c-9e66-749ac39bf764" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d13a9ee8-7991-5dcc-aded-17b229330d37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322166} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b2ec99-a23b-526e-b472-ba5af949b008" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29227877-bb5a-5e8d-a3d6-ef6fec4266a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105296" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120074 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82e4016c-e9e0-57b5-8062-ccca5dd23da5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117010} {:answer/id #uuid "95793e59-48c2-52e7-948b-d75ffe44549b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117011} {:answer/id #uuid "d86af066-4660-5ed8-bfdd-ece63aebb0da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117012} {:answer/id #uuid "41c0bea6-787a-5ece-84ee-d86aa4c21c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117013} {:answer/id #uuid "4eda4f57-4838-58b3-9271-3c47e10adc90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41c0bea6-787a-5ece-84ee-d86aa4c21c76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117013} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a65dbe9-5584-55fe-8006-984cd0184c94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d522f4eb-6fdb-5796-8d35-98401ee7b35b" :question/math-academy-id "q-156591" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-2{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-2[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+1-2 \\\\ 2+0-2 \\\\ 0+2-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{3}=-2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab75b4c-17da-5444-9d36-8dbc48b34ca2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322170} {:answer/id #uuid "a3144997-79a9-5c0e-836b-4a5901f98299" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322171} {:answer/id #uuid "29010734-784a-50e8-985b-948255417021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322172} {:answer/id #uuid "ced66730-8372-540b-b38b-040abfaa6665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322173} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4ac9eb-b7c1-552c-b87e-da4c32841f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322174}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4ac9eb-b7c1-552c-b87e-da4c32841f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322174} :answer-field/id #uuid "41e7f7c7-ea6c-5efe-81ab-5a3cc708a65c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e94b4a20-385d-5e83-8707-4f95b308f88c" :question/math-academy-id "q-157228" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+5{a}_{3} & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+5[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 20 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2+0+15 \\\\ -7-10+20 \\\\ 0+0+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can reorder the equations in this system as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322175 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "693719a3-5855-5321-bae5-94e98ddad226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322177} {:answer/id #uuid "b75e711a-49b8-59e5-8620-e019d9f300a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322178} {:answer/id #uuid "044a9220-0d6c-5685-b51e-1c27e1315630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} {:answer/id #uuid "56c9d955-67d7-5996-9600-7315bd613f3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322180} {:answer/id #uuid "4668bf80-276b-5a3d-a548-81d1c8ddc6e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "044a9220-0d6c-5685-b51e-1c27e1315630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b21f05b-bdbe-5680-b092-859d8ff62fff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b25a813d-a21f-58fa-aca7-4029e2f0126a" :question/math-academy-id "q-157238" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}+2{a}_{2}+3{a}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+2[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 10 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -9 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-8+6 \\\\ -1+10-9 \\\\ 7+2-9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2$ and ${x}_{3}=3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d71b40b0-e8d6-5e59-b2e7-a8580b6901c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322184} {:answer/id #uuid "912ab92d-f66d-5843-beea-b8dcbda01367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322185} {:answer/id #uuid "205ac95f-1b0b-50e4-91b8-56416bcc2b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322186} {:answer/id #uuid "1977c1cb-2e73-555c-a3cb-b9f32da82539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322187} {:answer/id #uuid "86ecb26e-12fc-5753-99f1-c70ebbc0c140" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912ab92d-f66d-5843-beea-b8dcbda01367" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0a1b2d7-5d17-56dd-96f6-bb402121e043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "61308fef-cc12-55cb-8db6-9a8210a2137f" :question/math-academy-id "q-157252" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}-{a}_{3} & =-6[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12-8-4 \\\\ -6+4+2 \\\\ -12+7+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since statement I is true, we have ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ for ${x}_{1}=6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1$ and ${x}_{3}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly *dependent*.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb5ca50-e9f3-5513-aff1-1ada8c225a42" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent Sets by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68f9be48-30e4-58fc-9277-1ceca4f28b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322191} {:answer/id #uuid "201324d9-d720-5194-9328-ef129b931d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322192} {:answer/id #uuid "21af2095-0034-5a00-81a9-7c23e81b5191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322193} {:answer/id #uuid "75aa8f21-f085-53b9-a068-098a3298a1b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322194} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8370bb-dc91-5ab8-a7af-08b6e06dbd48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68f9be48-30e4-58fc-9277-1ceca4f28b68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322191} :answer-field/id #uuid "fec6d755-71cf-545a-a2a2-e46b779d1897" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d21a9d6f-18a3-52ba-9db8-ae762597bea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-157253" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2}=0.$\n- ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ only if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n- The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{a}_{1}+3{a}_{2} & =-[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system using back substitution, we find that the solution is ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa48c2ef-6739-5f7b-a667-89d5b854ed93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322198} {:answer/id #uuid "334a1090-a221-51f5-a430-9432a42a14ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322199} {:answer/id #uuid "2d2aa51a-de1c-5c1b-b955-85e0af3e0887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322200} {:answer/id #uuid "5daf9d73-66ac-5596-9917-753ff547f54c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322201} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c06e7-b336-5f4a-ac66-dbcab92dae48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "334a1090-a221-51f5-a430-9432a42a14ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322199} :answer-field/id #uuid "50f65b73-479e-5e33-a602-56e029b6c128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94ea999a-510b-5589-a54e-c0a56661cff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-34142" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]=-3[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=-3{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{a}_{1}-3{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 3{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=3$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0af6cce-cd06-5758-a89a-6a51369fcda7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118025} {:answer/id #uuid "edb859c3-c8f8-529f-bd52-16a41793ca39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -10 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118026} {:answer/id #uuid "064fe1c8-24f4-5298-9309-5558161860fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-18 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118027} {:answer/id #uuid "20eda10f-a2d1-5cd2-8769-f3d86730499a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 15\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118028} {:answer/id #uuid "5cd631a4-527d-52a4-a2ad-53e14023cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cd631a4-527d-52a4-a2ad-53e14023cd9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118029} :answer-field/id #uuid "d94319c3-5829-5f1a-8376-0a32686004e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24ebfa1a-3835-5c3f-9168-f1537d003724" :question/math-academy-id "q-34291" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -20 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]=5[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=5{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{a}_{1}-5{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 5{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=5$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4ebe63-1468-518b-a60c-d24200234fee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322205} {:answer/id #uuid "3c919a80-b519-51d5-89e1-b3492a49f913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322206} {:answer/id #uuid "1c187017-06b7-514c-8361-b8dca95cc72c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322207} {:answer/id #uuid "481e7927-dcd2-50ce-9ceb-e6341505e824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322208} {:answer/id #uuid "00dccb05-6434-5240-9bb8-5e7442a7d7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c919a80-b519-51d5-89e1-b3492a49f913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322206} :answer-field/id #uuid "57eacdce-d0b1-503a-bc21-6b75907396a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9d69930-3b1b-589e-9811-11f0b215d63a" :question/math-academy-id "q-34700" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statement II is false, while statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af345a9c-c8c9-5256-8010-4b472e4f6ba8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322212} {:answer/id #uuid "520be6cd-36f8-59a8-8ec7-d9a341fa0c6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322213} {:answer/id #uuid "dab777aa-8765-54dd-8c29-bbed47936c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322214} {:answer/id #uuid "4407309b-da06-510a-ae51-f1b6d8d73885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322215} {:answer/id #uuid "d97998b3-5da3-5dc1-a46e-09061309f5c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dab777aa-8765-54dd-8c29-bbed47936c38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322214} :answer-field/id #uuid "433dcd0c-416a-5791-acf0-65c2b0fcfbbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "33decf41-3bd4-578c-872e-64d9973102cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-34714" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements I and III are true, while statement II is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d06480f0-4ca1-5982-9636-b8d64f589ef8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322219} {:answer/id #uuid "ee105e47-e907-523a-8237-f807ea1cdf85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322220} {:answer/id #uuid "da10b9e5-0c92-5b01-8c6a-e99dc823249d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322221} {:answer/id #uuid "6b944d5e-ddee-5d5a-8c33-00f44a93c1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322222} {:answer/id #uuid "491a9cb9-2bf9-5567-bc79-0ddca5afe073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b944d5e-ddee-5d5a-8c33-00f44a93c1a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322222} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bbbc11-d134-5bc0-8d53-88ebcef14f48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a792333-1cd7-596e-9f36-ea2d3aa5343e" :question/math-academy-id "q-75640" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=4[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=4{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{a}_{1}-4{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{a}_{1}+(-1){a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=4$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5130f54d-2a53-5373-81a6-355921ea7523" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector That Makes a Set Linearly Dependent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af78d287-e340-5006-9cd0-12ccbefb9bec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322226} {:answer/id #uuid "348fd172-f1b2-5353-a075-bcbe10f768d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322227} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a83bd4-85a4-55da-9c83-3d4a142ad6a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322228} {:answer/id #uuid "81f112b1-7432-568f-925a-18606d9533bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322229} {:answer/id #uuid "22432655-8b0e-5ea7-a885-2a6d80e26128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81f112b1-7432-568f-925a-18606d9533bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322229} :answer-field/id #uuid "15fa3198-c889-5712-bd2a-b24c7f7ffbd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1429f843-5ee1-5edb-b1a8-4da2570633ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-75645" :question/problem "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=-2[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=-2{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}-2{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ 2{a}_{1}+{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c811c5a6-5cd3-58bb-bf00-2384c1e614ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322233} {:answer/id #uuid "4333360f-20ad-542f-98ef-786b371e1808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322234} {:answer/id #uuid "29d3aa6a-195d-5043-92a7-e7df6631d160" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322235} {:answer/id #uuid "409c4654-243b-5edc-a7ab-97592ad4bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} {:answer/id #uuid "b6aa35f6-bc82-534e-9a99-1ac15de74659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "409c4654-243b-5edc-a7ab-97592ad4bf18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d0dbba-cef8-58b8-a620-82e924074bad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f3ee8a-560a-5203-b938-db996454917b" :question/math-academy-id "q-75655" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$" :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -6{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 12{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -4 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we conclude that the basic variables are ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$, while ${x}_{3}$ is the free variable. \n\nSince there is one free variable, the equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions, which means that the vectors are linearly dependent. \n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65b4743d-b76e-5065-bcd3-51eb341e107b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322240} {:answer/id #uuid "6651b604-6c7b-5284-8918-73ac57a5b134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322241} {:answer/id #uuid "70f2d002-7d01-5e72-b205-495e8b7dd009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322242} {:answer/id #uuid "41b83c2b-bd5e-55dd-a4ee-081ab6b03faa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322243} {:answer/id #uuid "a7b2a195-a635-528c-948b-d63d4dd3d8dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6651b604-6c7b-5284-8918-73ac57a5b134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322241} :answer-field/id #uuid "548962a3-2345-5e6f-aba2-898a9d3b149c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9f1717be-acd6-5282-be13-a9ce3bc21c5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75659" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has only the trivial solution ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}-2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are true, while statement I is false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322245 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "52a45814-d1e2-5948-b7bd-69dcaf6cd0eb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linearly Independent and Dependent Sets Using Row Reduction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "079aec00-9dc4-5043-8ce6-664a76ca6ad4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322247} {:answer/id #uuid "56372dc2-78b1-53d5-a7c4-d35dae773644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322248} {:answer/id #uuid "03cceddb-a80d-5f58-bcb2-7aba11645a9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322249} {:answer/id #uuid "319847eb-b03b-54e0-8fdb-e69d906cca7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322250} {:answer/id #uuid "176d085d-796f-56d6-9a81-15748c659f06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "319847eb-b03b-54e0-8fdb-e69d906cca7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322250} :answer-field/id #uuid "681f343d-4959-5326-b371-0ea77fbfe2ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322246}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fe061b30-cf59-50dd-9943-5f588993eca8" :question/math-academy-id "q-75660" :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 13 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent.\n- The vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly dependent.\n- The equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ has an infinite number of nonzero solutions." :question/worked-solution "We say that three vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}+{x}_{3}{v}_{3}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nThe equation ${x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}=0$ corresponds to the system \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}+13{x}_{2}-7{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}-4{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we create its augmented matrix and reduce it using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 13 & -7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 9 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nand, substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}-3(0)+2(0)=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}={x}_{3}=0$, we get that the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is linearly independent.\n\nTherefore, statements II and III are false, while statement I is true."}]}
